  "text": {
   "approach": "Reached from the notch between Chianti and Burgundy Spires (gained via a rappel from the west side per the original account, or by traversing in from Burgundy Col), then climbing the north face directly to the summit.",
   "approach_logistics": null,
   "road": "{\"name\":\"SR 20 (North Cascades Highway / Washington Pass)\",\"status\":\"Paved two-lane state highway; ordinary passenger cars, no forest road driving involved. Parking is on the highway shoulder at a wide pullout.\",\"driveNote\":\"From Washington Pass drive about 4 miles east to a broad pullout on the south (right) side of the highway near milepost 166, at roughly 4,250 ft - directly across the valley from Silver Star Mountain, roughly 0.7 mile past the Cutthroat Lake road and about 2 miles east of the hairpin turn. Several smaller pullouts appear first; the correct one has plenty of parking. From t",
   "overview": "The original 1952 first-ascent line up Chianti Spire, gained by rappelling into the Burgundy-Chianti notch and climbing the peak's north side on then-loose rock; now rarely repeated in favor of the East Face/Rebel Yell.",
   "description": null,
   "descent": "Downclimb or make a short rappel off the summit block back onto the north face, then reverse the line to the Burgundy-Chianti notch. From the notch, traverse north on snow beneath Burgundy Spire's east face and climb the 200-300 ft back up to Burgundy Col - this re-ascent is the sting in the tail and is much easier on firm morning snow than on afternoon slush or bare, sandy rock. From the col, des"
  },
